{"tool_name":"cn.mops","contents":["bioconda","bioconductor","biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:cn.mops","bioconda:bioconductor-cn.mops","bioconductor:cn.mops"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"bioconductor-cn.mops","version":"1.56.0","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/html/cn.mops.html","license":"LGPL (>= 2.0)","summary":"cn.mops - Mixture of Poissons for CNV detection in NGS data","identifiers":["biotools:cn.mops"],"description":"cn.mops (Copy Number estimation by a Mixture Of PoissonS) is a data processing pipeline for copy number variations and aberrations (CNVs and CNAs) from next generation sequencing (NGS) data. The package supplies functions to convert BAM files into read count matrices or genomic ranges objects, which are the input objects for cn.mops. cn.mops models the depths of coverage across samples at each genomic position. Therefore, it does not suffer from read count biases along chromosomes. Using a Bayesian approach, cn.mops decomposes read variations across samples into integer copy numbers and noise by its mixture components and Poisson distributions, respectively. cn.mops guarantees a low FDR because wrong detections are indicated by high noise and filtered out. cn.mops is very fast and written in C++.","license_file":"/lib/R/share/licenses/LGPL-3","build":{"number":0,"rpaths":["lib/R/lib/","lib/"],"run_exports":""},"requirements":{"build":[null,null,"make"],"host":["bioconductor-biobase >=2.70.0,<2.71.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-rsamtools >=2.26.0,<2.27.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","bioconductor-seqinfo >=1.0.0,<1.1.0","r-base","libblas","liblapack","zlib","liblzma-devel"],"run":["bioconductor-biobase >=2.70.0,<2.71.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-rsamtools >=2.26.0,<2.27.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","bioconductor-seqinfo >=1.0.0,<1.1.0","r-base"]},"source_url":["https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/cn.mops_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/Archive/cn.mops/cn.mops_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://bioarchive.galaxyproject.org/cn.mops_1.56.0.tar.gz","https://depot.galaxyproject.org/software/bioconductor-cn.mops/bioconductor-cn.mops_1.56.0_src_all.tar.gz"],"test":{"commands":["$R -e \"library('cn.mops')\""]},"parent_recipe":{"name":"bioconductor-cn.mops","path":"recipes/bioconductor-cn.mops","version":"1.26.0"}},"biotools":{"id":"cn.mops","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/fastseg","license":"LGPL-2.0-or-later","summary":"cn.mops (Copy Number estimation by a Mixture Of PoissonS) is a data processing pipeline for copy number variations and aberrations (CNVs and CNAs) from next generation sequencing (NGS) data. The package supplies functions to convert BAM files into read count matrices or genomic ranges objects, which are the input objects for cn.mops. cn.mops models the depths of coverage across samples at each genomic position. Therefore, it does not suffer from read count biases along chromosomes. Using a Bayesian approach, cn.mops decomposes read variations across samples into integer copy numbers and noise by its mixture components and Poisson distributions, respectively. cn.mops guarantees a low FDR because wrong detections are indicated by high noise and filtered out. cn.mops is very fast and written in C++.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T14:59:40Z","last_update_date":"2026-11-22T21:27:09.325130Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.58.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:cn.mops","credit":[{"name":"Alexander Blume","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Maintainer"]},{"name":"Guenter Klambauer","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"Sonali Kumari","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/fastseg"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Copy number estimation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3233"}]}],"name":"cn.mops","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gks003","metadata":{"abstract":"Quantitative analyses of next-generation sequencing (NGS) data, such as the detection of copy number variations (CNVs), remain challenging. Current methods detect CNVs as changes in the depth of coverage along chromosomes. Technological or genomic variations in the depth of coverage thus lead to a high false discovery rate (FDR), even upon correction for GC content. In the context of association studies between CNVs and disease, a high FDR means many false CNVs, thereby decreasing the discovery power of the study after correction for multiple testing. We propose 'Copy Number estimation by a Mixture Of PoissonS' (cn.MOPS), a data processing pipeline for CNV detection in NGS data. In contrast to previous approaches, cn.MOPS incorporates modeling of depths of coverage across samples at each genomic position. Therefore, cn.MOPS is not affected by read count variations along chromosomes. Using a Bayesian approach, cn.MOPS decomposes variations in the depth of coverage across samples into integer copy numbers and noise by means of its mixture components and Poisson distributions, respectively. The noise estimate allows for reducing the FDR by filtering out detections having high noise that are likely to be false detections. We compared cn.MOPS with the five most popular methods for CNV detection in NGS data using four benchmark datasets: (i) simulated data, (ii) NGS data from a male HapMap individual with implanted CNVs from the X chromosome, (iii) data from HapMap individuals with known CNVs, (iv) high coverage data from the 1000 Genomes Project. cn.MOPS outperformed its five competitors in terms of precision (1-FDR) and recall for both gains and losses in all benchmark data sets. The software cn.MOPS is publicly available as an R package at http://www.bioinf.jku.at/software/cnmops/ and at Bioconductor.","authors":[{"name":"Bodenhofer U"},{"name":"Clevert DA"},{"name":"Hochreiter S"},{"name":"Klambauer G"},{"name":"Mayr A"},{"name":"Mitterecker A"},{"name":"Schwarzbauer K"}],"citationCount":396,"date":"2012-05-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic acids research","title":"cn.MOPS: mixture of Poissons for discovering copy number variations in next-generation sequencing data with a low false discovery rate."},"pmcid":"PMC3351174","pmid":"22302147"}],"topic":[{"term":"Molecular genetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3321"},{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"}],"validated":1},"bioconductor":{"name":"cn.mops","version":"1.58.0","license":"LGPL (>= 2.0)","summary":"cn.mops - Mixture of Poissons for CNV detection in NGS data","description":"cn.mops (Copy Number estimation by a Mixture Of PoissonS) is a data processing pipeline for copy number variations and aberrations (CNVs and CNAs) from next generation sequencing (NGS) data. The package supplies functions to convert BAM files into read count matrices or genomic ranges objects, which are the input objects for cn.mops. cn.mops models the depths of coverage across samples at each genomic position. Therefore, it does not suffer from read count biases along chromosomes. Using a Bayesian approach, cn.mops decomposes read variations across samples into integer copy numbers and noise by its mixture components and Poisson distributions, respectively. cn.mops guarantees a low FDR because wrong detections are indicated by high noise and filtered out. cn.mops is very fast and written in C++.","home":"http://www.bioinf.jku.at/software/cnmops/cnmops.html","author":"Guenter Klambauer [aut], Gundula Povysil [cre]","maintainer":"Gundula Povysil <povysil@bioinf.jku.at>","bioc_